TITLE="Which private wells to test first? Risk maps from other states fail, but local test results help"
P(TITLE,bold=True,al=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER).runs[0].font.size=Pt(15)
P("[Author names, affiliations, ORCID and corresponding-author details: to be added by the authors]",italic=True,al=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER)
hw=wm.mean(); 
H1("Abstract")
P(f"Health agencies can often test only some private wells for arsenic, so the order of testing matters. Machine-learning risk maps are the usual guide, but they are mostly scored with random splits. Using USGS arsenic results from {nst} US states, we simulated testing campaigns under a fixed budget and held out whole states. A random split gave ROC-AUC {rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.2f}; held-out states gave {rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.2f}. With 20% of a state's wells tested, a map trained on other states found {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times as many unsafe wells as random order (not significant after correction; worse than random in {len(bad_static)} states). Re-ranking wells with the results of nearby wells tested earlier reached {s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order (about {u100['M4_neighbor_only']:.0f} unsafe wells per 100 tests against {rnd100:.0f}), and a rule that only follows unsafe results did as well ({sw.H1_follow_unsafe.mean():.2f}). Gains were larger where unsafe wells cluster in space (Spearman ρ = {XM['as10'].loc['lift_20km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f}). Adding the map to feedback did not help in a new state. When a state's own earlier wells were available, the map worked ({hw.W2_map_with_history:.2f}), and combining that history with feedback was best on average ({hw.W6_history_prior_plus_feedback:.2f}). Results were similar, but less certain, for a 5 µg/L threshold and for uranium. The wells are not a random sample and no prospective test was done.")
P("Keywords: groundwater arsenic; private wells; spatial validation; adaptive sampling; screening; transferability",italic=True)
H1("1. Introduction")
P("Arsenic in drinking water harms health. Private wells are tested at the initiative of owners or health agencies, not through routine public-supply monitoring, and agencies run outreach campaigns to get wells tested (CDC, 2026). Several studies use machine learning to map the chance that a private well exceeds an arsenic threshold (Lombard et al., 2021; Erickson et al., 2021; Azad et al., 2026), and this journal has published related work on groundwater arsenic and fluoride (Cao et al., 2024), on machine learning for groundwater quality (Haggerty et al., 2023) and on prediction with limited monitoring data (Zhang et al., 2024). These maps are valuable for exposure research. They are less clear as a guide for a testing campaign. The national private-well model of Lombard et al. (2021) reported a sensitivity of 33.9% at 10 µg/L on its test data, so most unsafe wells were missed at its decision cut-off.")
P("Two questions follow. First, how well does a risk map work where it has not seen data? Random splits can put neighbouring wells in both training and test sets. Spatial validation with buffers is reported to give lower scores for groundwater models (Appiah et al., 2025), and similar inflation appears in leave-one-country-out tests of crop yield models (Adjei, 2026). Second, a campaign is not a one-shot prediction. Results arrive while testing is under way, and nearby wells tend to share geology. Risk-based sampling designs for arsenic (Yin et al., 2021), active search for valuable regions (Active Area Search, AISTATS) and budgeted geospatial discovery (Khan et al., 2026) are related. We did not find a study that measures, on national data held out by state, how a map trained elsewhere compares with feedback from tested wells and with a state's own history.")
fig("figures/fig_story.png","Figure 1. The question studied here. An agency orders wells for testing, tests a batch, and may update the order from the results. Methods are scored by the unsafe wells found per 100 tests.",15)
P("We therefore simulate campaigns using the real arsenic results of USGS wells as the ground truth that testing would reveal. Our contributions are:")
for t in ("A campaign evaluation for well testing: methods are scored by unsafe wells found at a fixed budget, in units an agency can use, with states held out and a 25 km buffer.",
 f"A measure of how much random splits overstate map quality (ROC-AUC {rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.2f} against {rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.2f}; recall at 20% tested {rv.loc['random_5fold','recall_at_20']:.2f} against {rv.loc['leave_state_out','recall_at_20']:.2f}).",
 "A comparison of static maps, feedback rules, a simple heuristic and hybrids across 11 methods and 5 seeds, with paired tests across states, and an analysis of why feedback works (spatial clustering, weight on the map, regional differences).",
 "A warm-start test, in which each state's earlier wells are available, showing when a map becomes useful.",
 "Negative results, reported in full: a hybrid of map and feedback did not beat feedback alone in a new state, and a simple heuristic matched the kernel rule.",
 "A check on a second contaminant (uranium), with limited statistical power."):
    D.add_paragraph(t,style="List Number")
H1("2. Data and methods")
H2("2.1 Data")
P("We downloaded discrete water-quality results from the USGS Samples API on 22–23 November 2026, one request per state. For arsenic, California and Colorado timed out and are excluded; for uranium all states were retrieved. We kept results from wells, in water, in µg/L, and used the earliest valid sample per well. Non-detects were labelled safe, and non-detects whose detection limit was above the lower threshold were dropped. After filtering, arsenic had 53,294 wells in 47 states (9.97% above 10 µg/L; 17.3% above 5 µg/L); wells sharing coordinates were collapsed, leaving 50,139. Uranium had 28,117 wells in 49 states (5.1% above 30 µg/L; 13.6% above 10 µg/L). The only predictors a planner holds before testing are latitude, longitude and the USGS aquifer code (present for 42% of arsenic wells). Well depth was missing for every well and was not used.")
H2("2.2 Leakage audit and splits")
P("We collapsed wells that share coordinates (2,939 arsenic identifiers), used only the earliest sample per well, and used no result-derived predictor. The main split holds out one state at a time and removes from training every well within 25 km of the held-out wells. States need at least 100 wells and 15 unsafe wells to serve as test units. Eight states drawn at random before any model was run were used only for tuning. A second design clusters wells of evaluation states into 60 spatial blocks; a third keeps one well per 1 km cell to reduce clustered sampling.")
H2("2.3 Campaign simulation and scores")
P("In a held-out unit, wells are tested in batches of 10. Before testing, a method sees only the predictors; after each batch it sees the outcomes of the wells just tested. The score is the number of unsafe wells found when 5%, 10%, 20% and 30% of wells have been tested, divided by the number expected under random order. This ratio, the enrichment, is 1 for random order by construction. The primary measure is enrichment at 20%.")
H2("2.4 Methods compared")
P(f"M1 is a static LightGBM map trained on all wells outside the buffer. M2 is a static logistic regression on coordinates. M3 is a static nearest-neighbour prevalence map. M4 ranks untested wells by a Gaussian-kernel weighted share of unsafe results among tested neighbours (first batch random). M5 combines a prior probability p from M1 with the kernel evidence: score = (κp + a)/(κ + a + b), where a and b are kernel-weighted counts of unsafe and safe results among tested wells and κ sets the weight kept by the prior. M6 samples from the matching Beta distribution. M7 refits LightGBM with the tested wells at fixed checkpoints. Four ablations remove the prior (A1), remove shrinkage (A2), fix the bandwidth (A3) or replace the prior with M3 (A4). Tuning used the same budget for all methods: 20 random-search trials for LightGBM and a 5 × 5 grid of bandwidth and κ on the tuning states only (selected: {tun['h_kappa'][0]} km and κ = {tun['h_kappa'][1]}). Two added baselines need no map: H1 follows unsafe results only (rank by kernel-weighted count of unsafe neighbours), and H2 spreads tests evenly in space using no outcomes.")
H2("2.5 Added analyses")
P("To see why feedback works we measured, for each state, how strongly unsafe wells cluster: the chance that a well within r km of an unsafe well is also unsafe, divided by the state's share of unsafe wells (r = 2, 5, 20 km), and related it to feedback enrichment with Spearman correlation. We swept κ from 0.5 to 1000. In a warm-start design, the earliest half of each state's wells by sample date served as history and the campaign ran on the later half; maps were trained with or without other states' wells, and history could also seed the feedback rule. A regional check compared the feedback gain across US Census regions.")
H2("2.6 Statistics")
P("The unit of analysis is the held-out state (or block) after averaging seeds. We used paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, the median paired difference with a bootstrap 95% confidence interval (10,000 resamples of units), and Holm correction within each family of comparisons. Pre-registered choices and every later deviation are in the supplementary files; deviations 1–12 were recorded before the main runs finished and 13–15 before the added runs, with the reason for each.")
H1("3. Results")
H2("3.1 Random splits overstate map quality")
table(pd.DataFrame({"Split":["Random 5-fold","Leave-one-state-out (25 km buffer)"],"ROC-AUC":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_roc']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_roc']:.3f}"],"PR-AUC":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','auc_pr']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','auc_pr']:.3f}"],"Recall at 20% tested":[f"{rv.loc['random_5fold','recall_at_20']:.3f}",f"{rv.loc['leave_state_out','recall_at_20']:.3f}"]}),"Table 1. The same LightGBM model under two evaluations (mean of 3 seeds; As > 10 µg/L).")
fig("figures/fig_random_vs_blocked.png","Figure 2. Random splits versus held-out states for the same model.",13)
P("Under random splitting the map ranks unsafe wells well. Under held-out states the ranking is weak. About 10% of wells are unsafe, so a PR-AUC of 0.15 is only modestly above chance.")
H2("3.2 In a new state, a map from other states is close to random order")
P(f"At 20% of wells tested, the static map found {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times the unsafe wells of random order (95% CI {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_lo']:.2f}–{s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_ci_hi']:.2f}). Against random order, the {cmp(X['as10'],'M1_static_lgbm','M0_random(=1)')}. It was below random order in {len(bad_static)} of {nst} states ({', '.join(bad_static)}). The logistic regression was no better than random ({s10.loc['M2_static_logreg','enr_20_mean']:.2f}). For the 5 µg/L threshold the static map was {S['as5'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order.")
H2("3.3 Feedback from tested wells helps")
P(f"Neighbour-only feedback reached {s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order (95% CI {s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_ci_lo']:.2f}–{s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_ci_hi']:.2f}). Compared with the static map, the {cmp(X['as10'],'M4_neighbor_only','M1_static_lgbm')}. A campaign that tested 20% of wells found about {u100['M4_neighbor_only']:.1f} unsafe wells per 100 tests with feedback, {u100['M1_static_lgbm']:.1f} with the static map and {rnd100:.1f} with random order. Enrichment at 5% tested was {s10.loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_5']:.2f} for feedback and {s10.loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_5']:.2f} for the static map.")
fig("figures/fig_enrichment_as10.png","Figure 3. Enrichment at 20% budget for each held-out state (dots) and the mean (bar). The dashed line is random order.",14)
H2("3.4 A simple heuristic does as well; the map adds nothing in a new state")
P(f"The heuristic that only follows unsafe results (H1) reached {sw.H1_follow_unsafe.mean():.2f}; against the kernel rule, the {cx(XS['as10'],'H1_follow_unsafe','M4')}. Spreading tests evenly without using outcomes (H2) gave {sw.H2_spread.mean():.2f}, not better than random order. The hybrid (M5) reached {s10.loc['M5_proposed','enr_20_mean']:.2f} and did not differ from feedback alone: {cmp(X['as10'],'M5_proposed','M4_neighbor_only')}. Removing the prior (A1) gave {s10.loc['A1_no_prior','enr_20_mean']:.2f}. Refitting online (M7) gave {s10.loc['M7_online_refit','enr_20_mean']:.2f}, and Thompson sampling (M6) {s10.loc['M6_thompson','enr_20_mean']:.2f}; neither differed from M5 after correction. Because the weight on tested wells in M7 was fixed at 20 and not tuned, we checked weights 1, 20 and 100 afterwards (3 seeds): mean enrichment {W7.loc[1.0]:.2f}, {W7.loc[20.0]:.2f} and {W7.loc[100.0]:.2f}; none differed from M5 (unadjusted Wilcoxon p ≥ {WP7:.2f}), and the best of the three is an upper bound chosen after seeing results. With the 5 µg/L threshold the prior was harmful: M5 reached {S['as5'].loc['M5_proposed','enr_20_mean']:.2f} against {S['as5'].loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} for feedback alone ({cmp(X['as5'],'M5_proposed','M4_neighbor_only')}).")
H2("3.5 Why feedback works, and where it does not")
P(f"Sweeping κ gives a smooth path from feedback to the static map: enrichment fell from {sw['M5_kappa0.5'].mean():.2f} at κ = 0.5 to {sw['M5_kappa1000'].mean():.2f} at κ = 1000, and the smallest κ was best ({cx(XS['as10'],'M5_kappa0.5','M5_kappa1000')}). In other words, the more weight the map receives in a new state, the worse the campaign. Feedback gains were larger in states where unsafe wells cluster in space: the Spearman correlation between clustering within 20 km and feedback enrichment was {XM['as10'].loc['lift_20km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f} ({pf(XM['as10'].loc['lift_20km','p1'])}, n = {int(XM['as10'].loc['lift_20km','n'])}); for 5 µg/L it was {XM['as5'].loc['lift_20km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f}. Clustering within 2 km was not clearly related ({XM['as10'].loc['lift_2km','rho_vs_feedback_enrichment']:.2f}). The gain over the static map differed by region: median differences were {', '.join(f'{r} {RG['as10'].loc[r,'median_diff']:+.2f} ({int(RG['as10'].loc[r,'states_positive'])}/{int(RG['as10'].loc[r,'n'])} states positive)' for r in RG['as10'].index)}. The South stands out, with the weakest and least consistent gain for the 10 µg/L threshold. The static map was below random order in {', '.join(bad_static)}, and M5 was below random order in {', '.join(bad_ad)}; Minnesota was the clearest failure ({per.loc['MN','M1_static_lgbm']:.2f} for the map, {per.loc['MN','M5_proposed']:.2f} for M5). We did not test why, but arsenic in glacial aquifers may vary over short distances, which would weaken the idea that neighbours look alike; this is a hypothesis, not a result.")
fig("figures/fig_kappa_sweep.png","Figure 4. More weight on the map (larger κ) lowers enrichment in new states. Dotted line: the heuristic that follows unsafe results only.",14)
fig("figures/fig_mechanism.png","Figure 5. States where unsafe wells cluster more in space gain more from feedback (As > 10 µg/L).",9)
H2("3.6 With a state's own history, the map becomes useful")
P(f"In the warm-start design ({len(wm)} states for 10 µg/L), a map trained on other states alone reached {hw.W1_map_outside_only:.2f}. Adding the state's own earlier wells raised it to {hw.W2_map_with_history:.2f} ({cx(XW['as10'],'W2_map_with_history','W1_map_outside_only')}). A map trained on the state's own history only reached {hw.W3_map_local_history_only:.2f}, and cold feedback {hw.W5_cold_feedback:.2f}. The best method used history to form a prior and then applied feedback ({hw.W6_history_prior_plus_feedback:.2f}), which beat cold feedback ({cx(XW['as10'],'W6_history_prior_plus_feedback','W5_cold_feedback')}). For 5 µg/L ({len(wm5)} states) the pooled map was weaker than the local-only map ({wm5.W2_map_with_history.mean():.2f} against {wm5.W3_map_local_history_only.mean():.2f}), and history plus feedback again led on average ({wm5.W6_history_prior_plus_feedback.mean():.2f}). Local data therefore matter more than data from other states.")
fig("figures/fig_warm_start.png","Figure 6. Warm start: enrichment at 20% budget on each state's later wells. Bars show means; whiskers are approximate 95% intervals over states.",15)
H2("3.7 Robustness and a second contaminant")
tab=pd.read_csv("tables/main_table.csv"); NAMES={'M1_static_lgbm':'M1 static LightGBM map','M3_static_knn':'M3 static nearest-neighbour map','M4_neighbor_only':'M4 neighbour feedback only','M5_proposed':'M5 prior + feedback','M7_online_refit':'M7 online refit','A1_no_prior':'A1 hybrid without prior'}
tab["v"]=tab.enr20.map("{:.2f}".format)+" ["+tab.lo.map("{:.2f}".format)+", "+tab.hi.map("{:.2f}".format)+"]"; tb=tab.pivot(index="method",columns="scenario",values="v").rename(index=NAMES).reset_index().rename(columns={"method":"Method"})
tb.loc[len(tb)]=["Number of units"]+[int(tab[tab.scenario==c].units.iloc[0]) for c in tb.columns[1:]]
table(tb,"Table 2. Enrichment at 20% budget, mean [95% CI over units]; random order = 1. Units are states, or blocks where stated.")
P(f"In within-region blocks ({int(tab[tab.scenario=='As>10, spatial blocks'].units.iloc[0])} blocks) the static map was {S['as10_blocks'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order and feedback {S['as10_blocks'].loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f}; the {cmp(X['as10_blocks'],'M4_neighbor_only','M1_static_lgbm')}. After thinning to one well per 1 km cell, feedback found {S['as10_thin'].loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} times random order ({cmp(X['as10_thin'],'M4_neighbor_only','M0_random(=1)')}). Its edge over the static map was {row(X['as10_thin'],'M4_neighbor_only','M1_static_lgbm').median_diff_enr20:+.2f} but {pf(row(X['as10_thin'],'M4_neighbor_only','M1_static_lgbm').holm_p)} after correction, so we do not claim a significant gain over the static map in thinned data. For uranium, the static map reached {S['u30'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} (30 µg/L, 9 states) and {S['u10'].loc['M1_static_lgbm','enr_20_mean']:.2f} (10 µg/L, 12 states), and feedback {S['u30'].loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f} and {S['u10'].loc['M4_neighbor_only','enr_20_mean']:.2f}. The direction matches arsenic, but with so few states most differences were not significant after correction (feedback against random order at 30 µg/L: Holm-adjusted {pf(row(X['u30'],'M4_neighbor_only','M0_random(=1)').holm_p)}). We treat uranium as supporting direction only. Variation across seeds was small (SD of mean enrichment for M5: {pd.read_csv('tables/seed_variability_as10.csv',index_col=0).loc['M5_proposed','std']:.2f}).")
fig("figures/fig_generality.png","Figure 7. Enrichment at 20% budget across scenarios (mean and 95% CI over units).",15.5)
H2("3.8 Negative control and cost")
P(f"To check that the gain does not come from a flaw in our code, we shuffled outcomes within each state and ran neighbour feedback (5 shuffles per state). Enrichment was {XC['negative_control_M4_enrichment20_mean']:.2f} (95% CI {XC['ci'][0]:.2f}–{XC['ci'][1]:.2f}), as expected for random order. In {XC['timing_state']} ({XC['timing_n_wells']:,} wells; one core) fitting and scoring the static map took {XC['timing']['M1_fit_and_score_s']:.1f} s, a neighbour-feedback campaign {XC['timing']['M4_campaign_s']:.1f} s and the online-refit method {XC['timing']['M7_online_refit_s']:.1f} s, about {XC['timing']['M7_online_refit_s']/XC['timing']['M4_campaign_s']:.0f} times the feedback rule with no better result.")
H1("4. Discussion")
P("For an agency starting in a state with no data, the practical message is to plan for feedback. A rule as simple as ‘test near wells that just came back unsafe’ raised the number of unsafe wells found per test by about 80% over random order in our simulation, and it beat a map trained on other states. Even-spread sampling did not help. Following hot spots is an intuitive idea; our contribution is to measure its size against the maps that the literature produces, on national data held out by state.")
P("The map is not useless, but where it is trained matters. The warm-start results suggest that a state's own history is worth more than a national pool: adding other states' wells did not help for 10 µg/L and hurt for 5 µg/L. The best average result came from using local history as a prior and continuing to learn during the campaign, although its edge over the history-trained map alone was not significant for 10 µg/L. A plausible reading, which we did not test, is that arsenic depends on local geology that a model with only coordinates cannot learn from other states. The stronger gains where unsafe wells cluster at tens of kilometres, and the weak gains in some regions, fit this picture, but we did not test geological explanations.")
P("The negative results are part of the finding. The hybrid did not help in a new state, and the simple heuristic matched the kernel rule. One possible reason, which we did not test, is that the prior carries regional patterns that do not transfer and the kernel rule corrects for this only slowly. Other ways of combining the two, or maps that include geology, may do better.")
H1("5. Limitations")
for t in ("The wells are USGS study wells, not a random sample of private wells. Studies often sample clusters of wells, which makes neighbours more alike than in a real campaign. Thinning reduced but did not remove this concern. A prospective test is needed.",
 "We simulate campaigns on wells that already have results. Real campaigns also face refusals, cost differences and access limits, and we do not model the money cost of a test.",
 "Predictors are limited to location and aquifer code. Geology, soil chemistry and well construction matter and were not used; a map with them could do better.",
 "The warm-start split by sample date puts history wells close to the new wells in space, which favours history-based methods. It tests a realistic case (an agency with past results) but not a hold-out of locations.",
 "California and Colorado are missing for arsenic, nitrate could not be downloaded, and uranium has only 9 and 12 evaluation states. Generality beyond arsenic is therefore suggestive only.",
 "Held-out states are not fully independent because neighbouring states share aquifers. Tuning used 8 states set aside at random whose wells still served as training data for other states; this is not a full nested design. The clustering analysis is correlational with 29 states.",
 "The random-versus-held-out comparison used 3 seeds and an unbuffered random split.",
 "Related work exists (risk-based arsenic sampling, active search, spatial validation). Our novelty claim is limited to the empirical comparison reported here. Our literature search was a limited web search, not a systematic review."):
    D.add_paragraph(t,style="List Bullet")
H1("6. Conclusions")
P("In a new state, an arsenic risk map trained on other states ranked wells little better than random order, although a random split suggested otherwise. Testing wells in an order that follows recent results found about 1.8 times as many unsafe wells as random order, and a simple heuristic did as well as a kernel rule. A state's own earlier wells made a map useful, and history combined with feedback worked best. Campaign plans should be judged by held-out, budgeted simulation, and prospective trials with agencies should follow.")
H1("Declarations")
for t in ("Data availability: USGS Samples API (https://api.waterdata.usgs.gov/samples-data), retrieved 22–23 November 2026; processed well tables, per-unit results and code are in the submission package.",
 "Code availability: `code/` (run_all.py reproduces the main tables and figures; unit tests included).",
 "Ethics: no human participants; public well-level data were used.",
 "Conflicts of interest and funding: [to be completed by the authors].",
 "Use of AI tools: an AI coding assistant (Claude Code) wrote analysis code and a first draft of this text. The authors must review all content and take responsibility for it before submission.",
 "Author contributions: [to be completed by the authors]."): P(t)
